208
00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:38.350
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

209
00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:41.350
<v Speaker 2>It's proven by absolute unyielding fidelity to the leader.

210
00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:47.630
<v Speaker 2>command when the situation is terrifying, when everyone else is fleeing, and when it seems logical to retreat.

211
00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:52.430
<v Speaker 2>The first imam demonstrated the constancy required to hold his assigned post.

212
00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:56.670
<v Speaker 1>And the man later elevated to power failed that exact test.

213
00:14:56.990 --> 00:15:00.750
<v Speaker 2>The verdict on holy struggle is written by their own actions.

214
00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:04.110
<v Speaker 1>The author then moves to the virtues of knowledge and piety.

215
00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:05.950
<v Speaker 1>linking them inextricably.

216
00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:14.270
<v Speaker 1>He details how the father of imams defined the hierarchy of these virtues, arguing that true piety is impossible without profound knowledge.

217
00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:17.630
<v Speaker 2>The author quotes the first imam explaining this hierarchy beautifully.

218
00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:23.950
<v Speaker 2>Knowledge is superior to wealth because knowledge actively guards you, whereas you must expend effort to guard your wealth.

219
00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:28.350
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, wealth diminishes when you spend it, but knowledge actually multiplies when it is shared.

220
00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:29.230
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

221
00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:33.910
<v Speaker 2>And the profound point regarding piety is that piety fundamentally consists of knowing what is lawful

222
00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:35.150
<v Speaker 2>and unlawful.

223
00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:39.870
<v Speaker 2>You cannot be scrupulously pious in your actions if you lack the knowledge of the law.

224
00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:41.950
<v Speaker 1>So piety is simply applied knowledge.

225
00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:48.910
<v Speaker 2>Therefore, the individual possessing the most complete knowledge must inherently be the most capable of true piety.

226
00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:58.910
<v Speaker 1>The author argues that all three virtues, holy struggle, knowledge, and piety, converge at their absolute summit in one person, the commander of the faithful.

227
00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:02.030
<v Speaker 1>These virtues don't point to a committee or an electorate.

228
00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:05.150
<v Speaker 1>They point to a single, specific individual.

229
00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:08.510
<v Speaker 1>And the community was not left to deduce this on their own.

230
00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:10.670
<v Speaker 1>The designation was spoken aloud.

231
